---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { WAITLIST_CONSENT_VERSION } from '../consts';
const updated = 'September 28, 2026';

// Everyone who processes data for HaulQ. Keep this list in step with the
// services haulq-app/apps/api/src/env.ts actually wires up.
const PROCESSORS: Array<[string, string]> = [
  ['Render', 'Hosts the HaulQ servers and database.'],
  ['Cloudflare', 'Hosts this website, stores waitlist entries, and stores uploaded documents.'],
  ['Clerk', 'Handles sign-in, passwords and account identity.'],
  ['Stripe', 'Takes subscription payments. Card details go to Stripe, never to HaulQ.'],
  ['Postmark', 'Sends HaulQ’s own email (invitations, alerts) and receives paperwork forwarded to your HaulQ address.'],
  ['Unipile', 'Connects a carrier’s own Google or Microsoft mailbox, if they choose to.'],
  ['Microsoft Azure', 'Reads text from scanned documents and photos.'],
  ['Anthropic', 'Reads document text our own rules could not, to find fields like the rate or the stops.'],
  ['HERE Technologies', 'Maps addresses, plans truck routes, turns positions into a city and state, and finds nearby truck stops.'],
  ['Yelp', 'Finds repair shops near a location, when someone searches for one.'],
  ['Motive', 'Supplies truck positions, only for carriers who connect their Motive account.'],
  ['Apple', 'Distributes the iPhone app and delivers notifications, if you allow them.'],
];
---
<Base title="Privacy" description="What HaulQ collects, why, who processes it, and how to have it removed.">
  <section class="relative overflow-hidden bg-ink text-white">
    <div class="hq-stripes absolute inset-0" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <div class="relative mx-auto max-w-3xl px-5 py-20">
      <h1 class="text-4xl font-semibold sm:text-5xl">Privacy</h1>
      <p class="mt-5 text-lg text-slate-300">
        Short version: HaulQ holds what a carrier needs to run its loads. That means its trucks,
        drivers, brokers, loads, paperwork and invoices, plus where a truck is while a load is
        moving. We use it to run HaulQ for that carrier and for nothing else. We don’t sell it,
        rent it, advertise with it, or let anyone train AI models on it.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-mute">Last updated {updated}</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mx-auto max-w-3xl space-y-12 px-5 py-16">
    <div>
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">Who this covers</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-slate">
        It covers four groups: people who visit this website, carriers and their team members who use
        HaulQ on the web at app.haulq.ai or in the HaulQ iPhone app, drivers invited by a carrier, and
        brokers who open a tracking link a carrier sends them.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-slate">
        Most of what is in HaulQ is put there by a carrier about its own business: its drivers, the
        brokers it hauls for, and its loads. For that information we act on the carrier’s behalf. If
        you are a driver or a broker with a question about what a particular carrier holds on you,
        start with that carrier. You can always reach us at
        <a class="font-medium text-brand underline underline-offset-2" href="mailto:privacy@haulq.ai">privacy@haulq.ai</a>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div>
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">Your account</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-slate">
        To use HaulQ you sign in with an email address and a password, or with the sign-in option
        shown on the screen. Clerk runs sign-in for us, so your password is never stored by HaulQ. We
        keep your name, your email address, which carriers you belong to, and your role in each one
        (owner, dispatcher, accountant or driver).
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-slate">
        Carriers pay for HaulQ on the web through Stripe. Stripe collects the card or bank details.
        HaulQ keeps only the plan, the subscription status, and the reference Stripe gives us. The
        iPhone app does not sell anything.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div>
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">What carriers put into HaulQ</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-slate">
        A carrier’s identity (name, MC and DOT numbers, contact details), its trucks and their
        dimensions, its operating costs, and the people on its team. It also holds the carrier’s drivers
        and their credential expiry dates, its brokers and their contact details, and its loads with
        their stops, rates and status. Invoices, payments and factoring records go in too, along with
        any load history the carrier imports from a spreadsheet.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-slate">
        HaulQ records who did what and when on each of these, so a carrier can always see how a
        number came to be. That history is kept as a permanent record, which is why some of it is
        retained after an account is deleted (see “Deleting your account” below).
      </p>
    </div>

    <div>
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">Paperwork</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-slate">
        Documents reach HaulQ in three ways. Someone uploads them or photographs them in the app. Or
        someone forwards them to the carrier’s HaulQ address. Or they arrive in a mailbox the carrier
        has connected. We store the file, who it came from, and when.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-slate">
        HaulQ reads each document to pull out details like the broker, the load number, the rate and
        the stops. Most documents are read on our own servers. A scanned page or a photo is sent to
        Microsoft Azure to read its text. When our own rules can’t find a detail, the document’s text
        is sent to Anthropic’s AI model. Both do this for us under terms that don’t allow them to use
        the content to train their models. Whatever a model finds is checked against the document’s own
        text before HaulQ uses it.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div>
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">A connected mailbox</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-slate">
        A carrier can choose to connect its own Google or Microsoft mailbox through Unipile. You sign in
        on Google’s or Microsoft’s own page, so HaulQ never sees the mailbox password. When a new email
        arrives, HaulQ takes its attachments and the sender’s address and treats them as incoming
        paperwork. We don’t store the rest of the message.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-slate">
        If the carrier turns it on, HaulQ Autopilot can also send email from that mailbox, such as
        payment reminders and invoices. Sending is off until the carrier switches it on, and it switches
        off again whenever the mailbox is reconnected. The carrier chooses, for each kind of message,
        whether HaulQ only shows a preview, asks first, or sends on its own. Every message HaulQ sends
        or drafts is kept word for word, with a record of its attachments, so the carrier can see
        exactly what went out. A carrier can disconnect the mailbox at any time.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div>
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">Location, camera and notifications in the iPhone app</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-slate">
        The app asks for your location only while you are using it, never in the background. It reads
        your position at three moments. When you check in at a stop. When you choose to share your
        position on a load. And when you search for repair shops near where you are. Check-ins and
        shared positions are saved against that load. A repair-shop search sends the position to Yelp
        for that search and is not saved by HaulQ.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-slate">
        The carrier that assigned the load sees your check-in times and position. If the carrier
        creates a tracking link for a broker, the broker sees the load’s status, the city and state the
        truck was last in (not an exact point), how recent that is, and the check-in times. The broker
        sees nothing else about you. If the carrier has connected Motive, truck positions can also come
        from Motive instead of, or as well as, the app.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-slate">
        The camera is used only when you tap to photograph paperwork. If you allow notifications, we
        store your device’s notification token so Apple can deliver HaulQ’s alerts to you. You can turn
        location, camera and notifications off at any time in your iPhone’s Settings.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div>
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">Broker checks</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-slate">
        HaulQ Verify and the free MC lookup on this site read public FMCSA records about a broker or
        carrier: authority, insurance filings and operating status. Each fact is stored with where it
        came from and when.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div>
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">What this website collects</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-slate">
        When you join the list for product updates, we store the email address you give us. We also
        store any optional details you add: an MC or DOT number, your fleet size, your equipment type,
        and which products interest you. With that we record when you signed up, the page you signed up
        from, the country your request came from, and a version tag for the consent wording that was on
        screen at the time (currently <code class="rounded bg-wash px-1.5 py-0.5 text-sm">{WAITLIST_CONSENT_VERSION}</code>).
        The tag lets us show you exactly what you agreed to. We use this to email you about HaulQ. If we
        ever want to email you about anything else, we will ask first.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-slate">
        The MC lookup needs no account, and we don’t link a lookup to you. The load profit calculator
        runs entirely in your browser, so its numbers are never sent anywhere. The contact form stores
        what you write so we can answer it.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div>
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">Who processes data for us</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-slate">
        Each of these companies acts for HaulQ and may only use the data to provide its service to us.
      </p>
      <dl class="mt-5 divide-y divide-line rounded-2xl border border-line">
        {PROCESSORS.map(([name, role]) => (
          <div class="grid gap-1 px-5 py-3.5 sm:grid-cols-[11rem_1fr] sm:gap-4">
            <dt class="font-semibold text-ink">{name}</dt>
            <dd class="text-sm text-slate">{role}</dd>
          </div>
        ))}
      </dl>
      <p class="mt-4 text-slate">
        There are no advertising trackers, analytics scripts or third-party cookies on this website or
        in the app. Nothing is sold or shared with advertisers or data brokers.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div>
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">How long we keep it</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-slate">
        A carrier’s records, including check-ins, positions, documents and sent messages, are kept for as
        long as the carrier uses HaulQ. They become part of each load’s history and are not deleted on a
        timer. If a carrier leaves HaulQ, it can ask us to delete its data. We will, apart from anything
        the law requires us to keep, such as payment records.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-slate">
        Entries on the product-update list are kept until you ask us to remove them or until we retire
        the list. If you unsubscribe, we keep a record of that, so we don’t email you again by mistake.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div>
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">Deleting your account</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-slate">
        You can delete your HaulQ login from the account menu on the web, or from the iPhone app, which
        opens the same page. This deletes your sign-in right away. Your name stays on the actions you
        took, such as a check-in or an approved invoice. That keeps the carrier’s records accurate for
        everyone else who relies on them. The carrier’s own business records are not deleted with your
        login, because they belong to the carrier.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div>
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">Your choices</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-slate">
        Email
        <a class="font-medium text-brand underline underline-offset-2" href="mailto:privacy@haulq.ai">privacy@haulq.ai</a>
        to ask what we hold about you, to get a copy of it, to correct it, or to have it removed. We will
        do it, and we won’t ask you to explain why. If the information belongs to a carrier’s records,
        we will work with that carrier to answer you. Every marketing email also has an unsubscribe link.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-slate">
        HaulQ is a business tool for trucking companies and is not meant for children.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div>
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold">Changes</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-slate">
        If this policy changes in a way that affects what we do with your information, we will update the
        date at the top. If the change is material, we will also tell carriers using HaulQ and the people on
        our list before it takes effect.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
